<section xml:id="Section-1">

       <title>Bases</title>

      <p>
        A <term>basis</term> of a vector space <m>V</m> is a subset of <m>V</m> that is both linearly independent and spanning.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-finite-spanning-set-has-basis">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Any vector space <m>V</m> of the form <m>\operatorname{span}(S)</m>, where <m>S</m> is finite, has a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Choose a linearly independent subset <m>I\subseteq S</m> of maximal cardinality; this is possible because <m>S</m> is finite.
            If <m>I=S</m>, then <m>I</m> is both spanning and linearly independent, so it is a basis.
          </p>
          <p>
            Otherwise, let <m>\vec{s}\in S\setminus I</m>. Maximality of <m>I</m> implies that
            <m>I\cup\{\vec{s}\}</m> is linearly dependent. By <xref ref="ex-independent-extension"/>,
            <m>\vec{s}\in\operatorname{span}(I)</m>. Thus every element of <m>S</m> lies in
            <m>\operatorname{span}(I)</m>, so
          </p>
          <me>
            V=\operatorname{span}(S)\subseteq\operatorname{span}(I)\subseteq V.
          </me>
          <p>
            Hence <m>\operatorname{span}(I)=V</m>, and <m>I</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-where-finiteness">
        <statement>
          <p>Which step used finiteness?</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        A vector space that can be written as the span of finitely many vectors is called <term>finite-dimensional</term>.
        We call the size of a basis the <term>dimension</term> of a vector space. By the previous result, every finite-dimensional
        vector space has a finite basis, which is where the term comes from. However, this definition could hypothetically fail
        to make sense: if a vector space had one basis of size <m>2</m> and another of size <m>3</m>, what would its dimension be?
        Luckily, we can prove this cannot happen.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-base-size">
        <statement>
          <p>Every basis of a finite-dimensional vector space has the same finite size.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        To prove this, we will use a famous lemma that gives a way to swap elements of bases.
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-steinitz-exchange">
        <title>Steinitz Exchange Lemma</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> be two bases of a finite-dimensional vector space. For any
            <m>\vec{v}\in B_2\setminus B_1</m>, there exists <m>\vec{u}\in B_1\setminus B_2</m> such that
            <m>(B_1\setminus\{\vec{u}\})\cup\{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{v}\in B_2\setminus B_1</m>. Since <m>B_1</m> is a basis, there are distinct vectors
            <m>\vec{u}_1,\dots,\vec{u}_n\in B_1</m> and scalars <m>a_1,\dots,a_n</m>, not all zero, such that
          </p>
          <me>
            \vec{v}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i\vec{u}_i.
          </me>
          <p>
            Some <m>\vec{u}_i</m> with <m>a_i\neq 0</m> must lie in <m>B_1\setminus B_2</m>. Otherwise every
            <m>\vec{u}_i</m> with nonzero coefficient would lie in <m>B_2</m>. Since
            <m>\vec{v}\notin B_1</m>, the vector <m>\vec{v}</m> is distinct from all of these <m>\vec{u}_i</m>,
            and the displayed equation would give a nontrivial linear dependence among elements of <m>B_2</m>.
            Relabel if necessary so that <m>a_n\neq 0</m> and <m>\vec{u}_n\in B_1\setminus B_2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We claim that <m>(B_1\setminus\{\vec{u}_n\})\cup\{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis. First, it is linearly independent.
            Indeed, <m>\vec{v}\notin\operatorname{span}(B_1\setminus\{\vec{u}_n\})</m>: if it were, then
            <m>\vec{v}</m> would have an expression in the basis <m>B_1</m> with coefficient <m>0</m> on
            <m>\vec{u}_n</m>, contradicting uniqueness of basis coordinates since the displayed expression has
            <m>a_n\neq 0</m>. By <xref ref="ex-independent-extension"/>, replacing <m>\vec{u}_n</m> by
            <m>\vec{v}</m> therefore preserves linear independence.
          </p>
          <p>
            To show spanning, solve the displayed equation for <m>\vec{u}_n</m>:
          </p>
          <me>
            \vec{u}_n=\frac{1}{a_n}\left(\vec{v}-\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}a_i\vec{u}_i\right).
          </me>
          <p>
            Thus <m>\vec{u}_n\in\operatorname{span}((B_1\setminus\{\vec{u}_n\})\cup\{\vec{v}\})</m>.
            Every other element of <m>B_1</m> is already in the replacement set, so that set spans <m>B_1</m>, and hence spans
            <m>V</m>. Therefore <m>(B_1\setminus\{\vec{u}_n\})\cup\{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        Using the exchange lemma, we can prove <xref ref="thm-base-size"/>.
      </p>

      <proof>
        <title>Proof of the basis-size theorem</title>
        <p> Since the vector space is finite dimensional, it has a finite basis by the argument in the proof of 
        <xref ref="thm-finite-spanning-set-has-basis"/>. Let <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> be two bases, and 
        suppose for the sake of contradiction that <m>|B_1| &lt; |B_2|</m>. We may furthermore assume that <m>B_1</m> is finite.</p>
        
         <p>Let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>B_{2} \setminus B_{1}</m> of maximal size such that there exists 
        <m>T \subseteq B_{1} \setminus B_{2}</m> such that <m>|S| = |T|</m> and <m>(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S</m> is a basis.
         Such a set exists, since one can take <m>S = T = \emptyset</m>. Note that </p>

        <me>|(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S| = |B_{1}| &lt; |B_{2}|.</me>
        <p>Thus, there exists <m>\vec{v} \in B_{2} \setminus ((B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S)</m>. 
        Then by the Steinitz lemma, there exists <m>\vec{u} \in (B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S</m> such that 
        <m>(B_{1} \setminus (T \cup \vec{u})) \cup S \cup \{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis, a contradiction to the maximality of <m>S</m>.</p> 


      </proof>

      </section>